      "message": "(Qwen + GLM 5.3)\n\n# Review of commit `73704fc8` — \"mongo: change log schema\"\n\n**My overall opinion: the fundamental change is the right one** (removing the shared\n`log_id` counter in favor of locally generated `ObjectId`s radically eliminates the\ninsertion race, and deletes ~70 lines of fragile BSON code). **But the commit\nintroduces a real memory bug in the deletion path**, plus two migration/performance\nissues. Purely static review — I compiled and ran nothing, per your rule.\n\n---\n\n## 🔴 Introduced bug: OID lifetime in `mongo_backend_delete_logs`\n\nThis is the most serious point. The old code copied **values**:\n\n```c\nint64_t *ids;\nids[index] \u003d bson_iter_int64(\u0026iter);   // copies 8 bytes, owned by the array\n```\n\nThe new code stores **borrowed pointers** (`logs.c:279`, `:366`):\n\n```c\nconst bson_oid_t **ids;\nids[index] \u003d bson_iter_oid(\u0026iter);     // pointer INTO the cursor\u0027s document\n```\n\n**Why this is a problem**: `bson_iter_oid()` returns a pointer into the document\nbuffer returned by `mongoc_cursor_next()`. In mongo-c-driver, these documents are\n*views* (via `bson_init_static`) into the cursor\u0027s reply buffer — they are not\ncopies. As long as we stay within the same batch, each document\u0027s bytes remain in\nplace (distinct offsets in the same buffer), so the pointers coexist. But as soon\nas `options.count` exceeds the first batch (~101 documents by default), the cursor\nissues a `getMore` which **replaces** that buffer: the pointers collected from\nearlier batches become dangling (freed or overwritten memory).\n\nThe `$in` selector is built **after** the iteration loop (`logs.c:383-390`), then\n`delete_many` reads `ids[i]` (`logs.c:386`) — so every OID from an earlier batch is\nread too late: corrupted OIDs → `delete_many` matches nothing → **silently failed\ndeletion**, or undefined behavior if the buffer was freed.\n\n**Trigger condition**: `rbh_log --last 500 --delete` (or any count \u003e ~101) on a\ndatabase holding enough logs. This is precisely the GC scenario on a growing\ndatabase. The modified test cannot see it: it only has 7 logs, a single batch.\n\n**Minimal fix** (4 lines) — copy the OIDs into owned storage:\n\n```c\n-    const bson_oid_t **ids \u003d NULL;\n+    bson_oid_t *ids \u003d NULL;\n...\n-            ids[index] \u003d bson_iter_oid(\u0026iter);;\n+            bson_oid_copy(bson_iter_oid(\u0026iter), \u0026ids[index]);\n...\n-        if (!bson_append_oid(\u0026array, key, key_length, ids[i])) {\n+        if (!bson_append_oid(\u0026array, key, key_length, \u0026ids[i])) {\n```\n\n(`xcalloc(options.count, sizeof(*ids))` stays correct: `sizeof` becomes 12, the\nsize of a `bson_oid_t`.)\n\n---\n\n## 🟠 Migration: existing databases break deletion\n\nTwo combined consequences for a database created before this commit (old logs have\nint64 `_id` and **no** `logged_at` — the field was only added for new inserts by\n`27cb41c5`, with no backfill):\n\n1. **Sorting**: a missing field sorts as `null`, and `null` sorts before any date\n   ascending (after, descending). So the old logs end up grouped together, but\n   **in an arbitrary order among themselves** (all tied at `null`). `--first 50\n   --delete` no longer deletes \"the 50 oldest\" but 50 random old logs.\n\n2. **Type** (`logs.c:365`): `assert(BSON_ITER_HOLDS_OID(\u0026iter))` fails on an int64\n   `_id` → abort in debug builds. In release (`NDEBUG`), `bson_iter_oid()`\n   blindly interprets 12 bytes after the key → garbage OID → matches no document\n   → **old logs become impossible to delete, with no error message at all**.\n\nIf existing deployments matter, you need either a filter/guard or a migration. As\nit happens, an elegant migration exists:\n`db.log.updateMany({_id: {$type: \"long\"}}, [{$set: {logged_at: \"$_id\"}}])` —\nsince the old ids are small increasing integers, `logged_at \u003d id` preserves the\nexact historical order (BSON dates sorting after numbers, old logs still sort\n\"before\" the new ones ascending).\n\n---\n\n## 🟠 Performance: sorting on an unindexed field\n\nBefore: sorting on `_id`, indexed by default by MongoDB → index walk.\nAfter: sorting on `logged_at` (`logs.c:94` and `:303`) — yet **the plugin creates\nno index** (no `create_index` anywhere in `librobinhood`). Every `rbh_log` and\nevery `--delete` therefore does a full collection scan + in-memory server sort,\nwith the sort memory limit (~100 MB) beyond which the query outright fails —\nreachable on a database accumulating logs for years. A `{logged_at: 1, _id: 1}`\nindex created at backend init would fix this (and the next point).\n\n---\n\n## 🟡 Millisecond ties (the point your branch name hints at 😉)\n\n`logged_at` has millisecond precision (`logs.c:47`), and the sort relies on that\nfield alone. Yet MongoDB **does not guarantee a stable sort**: two logs inserted\nwithin the same millisecond — precisely the MPI case that motivates this commit —\ncome out in a non-deterministic order, and `--last N --delete` picks arbitrarily\namong the tied ones. This is not blocking (concurrent logs have no \"true\" order),\nbut it is not reproducible. Simple fix: add `_id` as a secondary sort key in both\n`opts` (get_logs and delete):\n\n```c\n\"sort\", \"{\",\n    \"logged_at\", BCON_INT32(options-\u003eascending ? 1 : -1),\n    \"_id\",       BCON_INT32(options-\u003eascending ? 1 : -1),\n\"}\"\n```\n\nNon-negligible bonus: for old logs (all tied at `null`), the `_id` tie-break on\nint64 **restores exactly the old ordering** — which also mitigates issue 1 above.\nAlso worth noting: `logged_at` comes from client-side `gettimeofday()`, so with\nMPI ranks spread over several nodes, the cross-node order reflects clock skew, not\nactual insertion (the old global counter did have a true total order) — an\nacceptable trade-off, but one to be aware of.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ The good parts\n\n- The commit message\u0027s diagnosis is correct and the solution is the right one: no\n  more shared read-modify-write state, a plain `insert_one`, every process inserts\n  independently.\n- Removing all the convoluted `find_and_modify` BSON parsing — a clear\n  maintainability win.\n- The test is properly adapted: dynamic ids via `get_last_log_id` instead of\n  hard-coded `1..7`, making it schema-independent. The shell helper\u0027s\n  `cut -d\u0027\"\u0027 -f4` is fragile but consistent with the existing style of\n  `db_cmd_mongo`, and the test\u0027s sequential commands are separated by far more\n  than a millisecond (no flake expected).\n\n## Nits (non-blocking)\n\n- `logs.c:57`: the \"Upsert failed\" message has become inaccurate, this is now an\n  `insert_one`.\n- `logs.c:13`: `#include \u003cunistd.h\u003e` added, but `gettimeofday()` is declared in\n  `\u003csys/time.h\u003e` (the file already compiled before via transitive includes) —\n  might as well use the correct header.\n- The counter document `{_id: \"log_id\"}` remains orphaned in the `info` collection\n  of existing databases — harmless (a few bytes).\n\n**In short**: I approve the direction, but I would block on the OID lifetime bug\n(4-line fix above, to be verified with a `--last 200 --delete` on \u003e200 logs since\nyour test infrastructure can do it), and the migration question needs to be\nsettled before merging.",
